 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np11-01",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Ablative absolute",
   "quote": "Antiocho fugato",
   "trans": "Antiochus having been routed",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The defeat of a great king is dismissed in two words, because Nepos's interest is Hannibal, not Antiochus.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-02",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "verens ne dederetur",
   "trans": "fearing that he would be handed over",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Hannibal's fear of surrender, already seen in chapter 7, drives the rest of his life.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-03",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Authorial comment",
   "quote": "quod sine dubio accidisset",
   "trans": "which without doubt would have happened",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Nepos steps in to confirm that the danger was real, so Hannibal's flight looks prudent rather than cowardly.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-04",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Superlative",
   "quote": "vir omnium callidissimus",
   "trans": "the cleverest man of all",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The superlative introduces a trickster story. Callidus means shrewd but also cunning, a word that fits Roman ideas about \"Punic\" cleverness.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-05",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Conditional",
   "quote": "nisi quid providisset",
   "trans": "unless he took some precaution",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The condition shows Hannibal thinking ahead, the quality that saves him in the trick that follows.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-06",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "propter avaritiam Cretensium",
   "trans": "because of the greed of the Cretans",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The Cretans are defined by greed, so the reader enjoys seeing the greedy outwitted.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-07",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Historic present",
   "quote": "capit … complet … operit",
   "trans": "he takes … fills … covers",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The switch to the present tense makes the trick vivid, as if we are watching Hannibal at work.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-08",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Antithesis",
   "quote": "complet plumbo, summas operit auro et argento",
   "trans": "fills them with lead, covers the tops with gold and silver",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Worthless lead inside, precious metal on top: the whole trick is caught in one contrast.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-09",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Alliteration",
   "quote": "praesentibus principibus",
   "trans": "in the presence of the leading men",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The repeated p sound draws attention to the witnesses, who are the very people being fooled.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-10",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "simulans se suas fortunas illorum fidei credere",
   "trans": "pretending to entrust his fortune to their good faith",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Hannibal pretends to rely on fides, the honesty the Cretans lack, so the trick turns their own vice against them.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-11",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Ablative absolute",
   "quote": "his in errorem inductis",
   "trans": "with them led into error",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The deception is finished in four words, and the story moves straight on to the real hiding place.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-12",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Vivid detail",
   "quote": "in propatulo domi abicit",
   "trans": "he throws them down in the open courtyard of his house",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Abicit, \"tosses aside\", shows the real treasure left carelessly in full view, which is the cleverest part of the plan.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-13",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Antithesis",
   "quote": "non tam a ceteris quam ab Hannibale",
   "trans": "not so much from others as from Hannibal",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The temple is guarded against its depositor, a comic reversal that shows how completely the Cretans have been fooled.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-14",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "ne ille inscientibus iis tolleret sua",
   "trans": "in case he took away his own property without their knowledge",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The Cretans guard jars of lead, while Hannibal's real wealth lies in his courtyard. Sua, \"his own things\", adds to the joke.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-15",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Pairing",
   "quote": "tolleret sua secumque duceret",
   "trans": "should take away his own property and carry it off with him",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Two verbs for removing the treasure show how anxiously the Cretans imagine Hannibal's escape.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-16",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Gortyn",
   "quote": "Gortynios",
   "trans": "the people of Gortyn",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Gortyn was one of the leading cities of central Crete, famous for its inscribed law code.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-17",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Cretans",
   "quote": "Cretensium",
   "trans": "of the Cretans",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The Cretans had a proverbial reputation for greed and dishonesty: the line \"Cretans are always liars\" is attributed to the Cretan poet Epimenides.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np11-18",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Diana",
   "quote": "templo Dianae",
   "trans": "the temple of Diana",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The temple of Artemis (Diana to the Romans). Temples were often used as safe places to deposit money and valuables.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 11,
 "questions": [
  "How does Nepos turn this episode into a story about character, both Hannibal's and the Cretans'?",
  "Why do you think Nepos includes such a light-hearted anecdote in a Life mostly about war and exile?",
  "Find the places where Nepos tells us what to think about Hannibal rather than letting the story speak for itself. How effective is this?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "Why Hannibal had to run",
   "body": "<p>After Antiochus was defeated at Magnesia (190 or 189 BC), the peace terms dictated by Rome included the surrender of Hannibal. He escaped before he could be handed over. Crete, a collection of independent cities with a reputation for piracy and mercenary service, offered a temporary refuge outside the reach of both Rome and the Seleucid king.</p>"
  }
 ]
}